# Contributing to Said

Thanks for considering a contribution. Said is built and maintained by [EMIAC](https://github.com/EMIAC-org), open-sourced under MIT, and designed to be infrastructure rather than a SaaS — so PRs that make it work for more people are very welcome.

## Quick start

```bash
git clone https://github.com/EMIAC-org/Said.git
cd Said
brew install just                  # task runner (or: cargo install just)
just dev                           # builds said-backend, launches Tauri dev
```

Run `just` (no args) to see every available task.

Pre-flight before opening a PR:

```bash
just check                         # fmt, clippy, test, typecheck — same as CI
```

## Tooling

| Need | Version |
|---|---|
| Rust | pinned in [`rust-toolchain.toml`](rust-toolchain.toml) — auto-installed by rustup |
| Node | 20+ (matches CI) |
| macOS | 13+ (Tauri minimum; the rebrand inherits this) |

The Rust toolchain pin includes `rustfmt` and `clippy`, so first-run `cargo` calls install both automatically.

## Project shape

```
crates/
  core           shared types + Deepgram bias model
  recorder       CoreAudio capture + ring buffer
  paster         Accessibility-driven cursor paste
  hotkey         Caps Lock / global hotkey via CGEventTap
  backend        local IPC backend (the Tauri sidecar)
  said           the standalone CLI binary
  control-plane  optional Fly.io coordination service (excluded from workspace)
desktop/         Tauri 2 + React 19 dashboard
landing/         Next.js marketing site
scripts/         dev tools (build-dmg.sh, bump-version.sh)
.github/         CI + release workflows + issue/PR templates
```

`control-plane` is **excluded from the Cargo workspace** because its sqlx-postgres feature collides with rusqlite's `libsqlite3-sys` linkage. Build it standalone with `cd crates/control-plane && cargo build`, or via `just clippy` which handles both.

## Coding style

- **Rust:** rustfmt is the source of truth. CI runs `cargo fmt --all --check` and rejects drift. `just fmt` re-formats everything in place.
- **Clippy:** CI runs `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets`. Correctness lints fail the build; style/perf lints surface as advisory. The full lint policy lives in `[workspace.lints]` in the root [`Cargo.toml`](Cargo.toml).
- **TypeScript:** `tsc --noEmit` gates the desktop and landing apps. Vite handles the build, so type errors otherwise hide.
- **Naming:** crate names are `said-*` (kebab); import paths are `said_*` (snake, Cargo-translated); the CLI binary and `vp` compat shim are both `said` at runtime.

## Commit messages

Follow the existing log style:

- Imperative, present tense, sentence-case.
- Title under ~70 chars, no scope prefix, no trailing period.
- Body wraps at ~75 cols, explains *why* not *what*.
- Include `Co-Authored-By` trailers when collaborating with another agent or person.

Examples from the log:

```
Hoist version metadata into workspace.package
Rename Cargo crates voice-polish-* → said-* (v2.0.0 prep)
Re-sort imports after said-* rename
```

## Pull requests

1. Fork, branch from `main`, make focused commits.
2. Run `just check` locally — CI runs the same gates.
3. Open the PR. The PR template prompts for summary, test plan, and breaking-change notes.
4. Small PRs land faster than big ones. If you're touching more than ~5 files, consider splitting.
5. If you're touching the rebrand surfaces (bundle ids, install dir, plist labels, command shims), call it out — those break existing v1.0.x users.

## What we'd love help on

- New STT provider adapters (Whisper, AssemblyAI, ElevenLabs, …)
- More Indic-language polish prompts (Tamil, Telugu, Bengali, Marathi, Punjabi)
- Linux & Windows ports (the hot path is Rust; the `crates/hotkey` and `crates/paster` macOS specifics need analogues)
- Domain-specific learning packs (legal, medical, code)
- Migration code for v1 → v2 Keychain entries (currently we wipe and ask the user to re-save)

Open an issue first for anything large so we can align on direction.

## Reporting bugs / security issues

- **Bug reports:** use the GitHub issue templates. Include `said doctor` output where possible.
- **Security:** see [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md). Please don't open a public issue for vulnerabilities.

